$DIR/main.p:3:23: cannot return make(chan int) as an interface: an ordinary interface only borrows the channel, whose reference f drops when it returns
    The chan int made at main.p:3 is a temporary: nothing keeps a reference to
    it after the statement, and an ordinary interface that lends it counts no
    reference of its own, so the interface may not outlive that statement.
    Converting make(chan int) to an ordinary interface lends it: the interface
    points at the channel without a reference of its own.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return the channel itself, changing the result type to chan int: the caller
    gets a reference of its own:
        return make(chan int)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
